\address[aff1]{School of Mechanical Engineering, Hanoi University of Science and Technology, No.~1 Dai Co Viet Road, Hai Ba Trung District, Hanoi, Vietnam} \begin{abstract}
Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) has been widely adopted for unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) path planning due to its conceptual simplicity and fast convergence.
However, its performance is highly sensitive to the choice of hyperparameters, which remain difficult to tune across heterogeneous environments.
This paper presents AFSACPSO, a self-adaptive PSO framework driven by a CrossQ-enhanced Soft Actor--Critic (AFSACPSO) agent that learns to regulate PSO parameters directly from swarm-level feedback.
A lightweight transformer encoder aggregates temporal patterns of swarm evolution, enabling the controller to reason over trends such as stagnation, dispersion, and convergence progression.
A simple fitness-improvement reward provides direct learning signals tied to changes in global best fitness.
Training stability and sample efficiency are further improved by adopting the CrossQ architecture, which eliminates target networks and employs batch-normalized critics.
The resulting controller produces continuous parameter values at each iteration, yielding fully data-driven adaptation without handcrafted schedules.
Experiments on three-dimensional terrain navigation benchmarks with no-fly zones show that AFSACPSO attains the best mean fitness in two of three RL scenarios, while online AFSACPSO attains the lowest variance across scenarios in our benchmark.
Against traditional self-adaptive PSO baselines, AFSACPSO remains competitive in fitness under a matched online evaluation protocol.
Ablation analyses on a refreshed 30-run protocol show scenario-dependent trade-offs across components, with no pairwise Holm-corrected differences reaching significance against the Full baseline.
These results indicate that CrossQ-enhanced Soft Actor--Critic constitutes a stable and practically deployable mechanism for adaptive PSO parameter control in UAV path planning, offering a favorable trade-off between solution quality and computational cost.
