\label{sec:problem} We consider the problem of planning a collision-free trajectory for a UAV operating in a 3D environment.
The environment is represented by a digital elevation map (DEM) $\mathcal{E}: \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ that returns terrain height for any $(x, y)$ coordinate, augmented with a set of cylindrical no-fly zones $\mathcal{O} = \{(c_j, r_j, h_j)\}_{j=1}^{M}$ representing threat areas.
The UAV must navigate from a start position $s_{\text{start}} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ to a goal position $s_{\text{goal}} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ while minimizing the fitness function defined in Equation~\ref{eq:fitness}. \begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/ProblemFormulation.pdf}
\caption{Geometric definition of UAV path planning parameters}
\label{fig:problem_formulation}
\end{figure} A candidate path is encoded as a sequence of $n$ waypoints $P = [p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_n]$ where each $p_i = (x_i, y_i, z_i) \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
The UAV follows linear segments between consecutive waypoints, yielding total path length:
\begin{equation}
L(P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \|p_{i+1} - p_i\|_2
\label{eq:path_length}
\end{equation} Path quality is evaluated through a weighted fitness function that balances competing objectives:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f(P) ={}& \lambda_1 L(P) + \lambda_2 C_{\text{turn}}(P) + \lambda_3 C_{\text{climb}}(P) \\
&+ \lambda_4 C_{\text{collision}}(P) + \lambda_5 C_{\text{alt}}(P)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fitness}
\end{equation} The turn penalty $C_{\text{turn}}$ penalizes sharp directional changes that exceed UAV maneuverability:
\begin{equation}
C_{\text{turn}}(P) = \sum_{i=2}^{n-1} \max\left(0, \theta_i - \theta_{\max}\right)^2
\end{equation}
where $\theta_i$ is the turn angle at waypoint $i$ and $\theta_{\max}$ is the maximum allowable turn. The climb penalty $C_{\text{climb}}$ penalizes excessive altitude changes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
C_{\text{climb}}(P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \max\Bigl(0, &\frac{|z_{i+1} - z_i|}{\|p_{i+1} - p_i\|_{xy}} \\
&- \gamma_{\max}\Bigr)^2
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_{\max}$ is the maximum climb/descent gradient. The collision penalty $C_{\text{collision}}$ combines terrain and obstacle violations:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
C_{\text{collision}}(P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Bigl[ &\max(0, \mathcal{E}(x_i, y_i) + h_{\min} - z_i) \\
&+ \sum_{j=1}^{M} \max(0, r_j + d_{\text{safe}} - d_{ij}) \Bigr]
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $d_{ij}$ is the distance from waypoint $i$ to obstacle $j$. The altitude cost $C_{\text{alt}}$ encourages the UAV to maintain low-level flight for terrain masking and energy efficiency:
\begin{equation}
C_{\text{alt}}(P) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} z_i
\end{equation} The weights $\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_5$ balance path efficiency against constraint satisfaction.
Lower fitness values indicate better paths. \section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods} The RL-based PSO parameter adaptation problem is formulated as a Markov Decision Process (MDP).
In the proposed algorithm, the MDP is defined by the tuple $(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{T}, \symGamma)$ as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:mdp_components}.
The state space $\mathcal{S}$ encodes swarm features over a temporal window.
The action space $\mathcal{A}$ represents PSO parameters $(w, c_1, c_2)$ applied globally to the swarm.
The reward function $\mathcal{R}$ provides direct feedback based on whether the swarm improves the current best fitness.
The transition rules $\mathcal{T}$ describe deterministic updates computed via the standard PSO velocity and position equations, functioning effectively as the environment transition function during learning.
The discount factor $\symGamma$ determines the temporal preference for cumulative returns. \begin{figure}[!htb] \centering \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/MDP.pdf} \caption{MDP formulation of AFSACPSO. At each PSO iteration $t$, the CrossQ-SAC agent observes the temporal swarm state $s_t$ (encoded by the Transformer), outputs continuous PSO parameters $(w_t, c_{1,t}, c_{2,t})$, and receives a fitness-improvement reward $r_t$ based on whether the swarm improves the current best path.} \label{fig:mdp_components}
\end{figure} \subsection{Transformer-based Temporal State Representation}
To address the limitations of static state observations used in prior RL-PSO methods, we propose a temporal encoding structure.
The following features characterize the swarm state at each iteration:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Iteration Progress: $\phi_1 = t / T_{\max}$
\item Population Diversity: $\phi_2 = \frac{1}{D} \sum_{d=1}^{D} \sigma(\mathbf{x}_{:,d}) / \|\mathbf{x}_{\max} - \mathbf{x}_{\min}\|$ \item Fitness Diversity: $\phi_3 = \sigma(f) / |\bar{f}|$ \item Stagnation Duration: $\phi_4 = (t - t_{\text{last\_improve}}) / T_{\max}$
\item Best Fitness: $\phi_5 = \tanh(\log_{10}(f_{\text{gbest}} + 1))$ \item Velocity Magnitude: $\phi_6 = \tanh(\bar{v} / \text{diameter})$ \item Convergence Speed: $\phi_7 = 1 - \tanh(\bar{d}_{\text{gbest}} / \text{diameter})$
\item Exploration Indicator: $\phi_8 = \phi_2 \times \phi_6$ \item Improvement Rate: $\phi_9 = 1/(1 + \Delta t_{\text{improve}})$ \end{enumerate} We aim to provide a multi-dimensional perspective on the swarm's search, moving beyond the simple state representation used in prior works. The combination of spatial diversity ($\phi_2$) and fitness-based diversity ($\phi_3$) allows the agent to detect structural stagnation before it impacts the global best. The inclusion of the exploration indicator ($\phi_8$) provides the agent with an understanding of the swarm's kinetic energy, which is crucial for determining whether to increase momentum or tighten the search around a local optimum. By log-scaling the best fitness ($\phi_5$) and tracking the recency of improvements ($\phi_9$), the encoder ensures that the agent remains sensitive to progress even in the late-stage "fine-tuning" phases of the optimization. The proposed feature set is collected over a temporal window of $\symWindowsize$ iterations, forming a feature matrix $F \in \mathbb{R}^{\symWindowsize \times \symFeaturecount}$.
A Transformer encoder \cite{Vaswani2017Attention} with multi-head self-attention processes this sequence to produce the $\symStatedim$-dimensional context vector $s \in \mathbb{R}^{\symStatedim}$, capturing long-range dependencies in swarm history that single-timestep MLP encoders cannot model. \subsection{Reward Function} The retained controller uses a direct fitness-improvement reward. Let $\Delta f = f_{\text{gbest}}^{t-1} - f_{\text{gbest}}^{t}$. The reward is defined as:
\begin{equation}
r_t =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } \Delta f > 0 \\
-1, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\label{eq:reward_total}
\end{equation}
This design keeps the reinforcement signal aligned with the optimization objective while avoiding extra reward-shaping terms. \subsection{CrossQ-Optimized Soft Actor-Critic} AFSACPSO employs Soft Actor-Critic \cite{Haarnoja2018Soft} with CrossQ optimizations \cite{Bhatt2024CrossQ} for learning the parameter adaptation policy.
SAC maximizes the entropy-regularized expected return: \begin{equation}
J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t \left(r_t + \alpha \mathcal{H}(\pi(\cdot|\mathbf{s}_t))\right)\right]
\label{eq:sac_objective}
\end{equation} where $\alpha$ is the temperature parameter (automatically tuned) and $\mathcal{H}$ is the policy entropy.
The algorithm maintains three components: Actor Network $\pi_\theta(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s})$: A Gaussian policy with learnable mean and diagonal covariance. The network architecture is $\mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{\symStatedim} \to \symHiddendimA \to \symHiddendimA \to \symHiddendimA \to (\boldsymbol{\mu}, \log \boldsymbol{\sigma}) \in \mathbb{R}^{2\symActiondim}$ with batch normalization after each hidden layer. Actions are sampled via reparameterization:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{a}_t = \tanh(\boldsymbol{\mu}_\theta(\mathbf{s}_t) + \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta(\mathbf{s}_t) \odot \boldsymbol{\epsilon}), \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I})
\end{equation} Twin Critic Networks $Q_{\phi_1}, Q_{\phi_2}$: Two independent Q-functions with architecture $[\mathbf{s}; \mathbf{a}] \in \mathbb{R}^{\symStatedim + \symActiondim} \to \symHiddendimA \to \symHiddendimA \to \symHiddendimB \to 1$. CrossQ incorporates batch normalization in critics for improved stability and eliminates target networks, instead using the current networks directly with update-to-data (UTD) ratio of $\symUtd$. The critic loss minimizes the Bellman error using the minimum of both critics:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
L_Q(\phi_i) = \mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a},r,\mathbf{s}')} \Bigl[ \bigl(Q_{\phi_i}(\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{a}) - (&r + \symGamma \min_{j=1,2} Q_{\phi_j}(\mathbf{s}', \mathbf{a}') \\
&- \alpha \log \pi_\theta(\mathbf{a}'|\mathbf{s}') )\bigr)^2 \Bigr]
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} Temperature Parameter $\alpha$: Automatically adjusted to match a target entropy $\bar{\mathcal{H}} = -\dim(\mathcal{A}) = -\symActiondim$ via:
\begin{equation}
L_\alpha = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{s} \sim \mathcal{D}}\left[-\alpha \left(\log \pi_\theta(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s}) + \bar{\mathcal{H}}\right)\right]
\end{equation} The key CrossQ modifications---batch normalization in all networks and UTD=$\symUtd$---significantly improve sample efficiency and training stability compared to standard SAC, enabling effective learning in the high-dimensional $\symActiondim$D action space. \subsection{Algorithm Workflow} The complete integration of the CrossQ-optimized SAC agent with the PSO search process is summarized in Algorithm \ref{alg:apexpso}. \begin{algorithm}[H] \caption{AFSACPSO Parameter Adaptation} \label{alg:apexpso} \begin{algorithmic}[1] \State \textbf{Input:} Swarm size $\symPopsize$, Max iterations $\symMaxIters$, Temporal window $\symWindowsize$, Replay buffer $\mathcal{D}$ \State \textbf{Initialize:} Swarm positions $x_i^0$, velocities $v_i^0$, Personal bests $p_i^0$, Global best $g^0$ \State \textbf{Initialize:} Actor $\pi_\theta$, Critics $Q_{\phi_{1,2}}$, State Encoder $E_\psi$, Temperature $\alpha$ \For{$t = 1$ to $\symMaxIters$} \State Compute swarm statistics $\Phi_t = [\phi_1, \dots, \phi_{\symFeaturecount}]$ \State Construct temporal state $s_t = E_\psi(\Phi_{t-\symWindowsize:t})$ \State Sample parameters $\mathbf{a}_t \sim \pi_\theta(\cdot|s_t)$ where $\mathbf{a}_t \in [-1,1]^3$ via $\tanh$ \State Map $\mathbf{a}_t$ to $\{w_t, c_{1,t}, c_{2,t}\}$ via affine rescaling: \Statex \hspace{2em} $w_t = 0.4 + 0.45\,\frac{a_t^{(1)}+1}{2},\quad c_{k,t} = 0.5 + 2.0\,\frac{a_t^{(k+1)}+1}{2},\; k\in\{1,2\}$ \Statex \hspace{2em} yielding $w_t \in [0.4,\, 0.85]$,\; $c_{1,t}, c_{2,t} \in [0.5,\, 2.5]$ \For{each particle $i \in \{1 \dots \symPopsize\}$} \State Update velocity $v_i^{t} \leftarrow w_t v_i^{t-1} + c_{1,t} r_1 (p_i^{t-1} - x_i^{t-1}) + c_{2,t} r_2 (g^{t-1} - x_i^{t-1})$ \State Update position $x_i^{t} \leftarrow x_i^{t-1} + v_i^{t}$ \EndFor \State Evaluate fitness $f(x_i^t)$ and update $p_i^t$ and $g^t$ \State Compute fitness-improvement reward $r_t$ (Eq. \ref{eq:reward_total}) \State Observe $s_{t+1}$ and store transition $(s_t, \mathbf{a}_t, r_t, s_{t+1})$ in $\mathcal{D}$ \If{training session active} \For{$j = 1$ to $\symUtd$} \State Sample minibatch $\mathcal{B} \sim \mathcal{D}$ \State Update Critics $Q_{\phi_{1,2}}$ using CrossQ Bellman loss \EndFor \State Update Actor $\pi_\theta$ and Temperature $\alpha$ \EndIf \EndFor \State \Return Optimal path $g^t$ \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm} \section{Results}
\label{sec:results} \subsection{Experimental Setup} Path quality is measured by global best fitness (Equation \ref{eq:fitness}), which combines path length, turning penalties, climbing penalties, and collision penalties.
Lower fitness indicates better paths. To establish a baseline for performance, we evaluate the proposed AFSACPSO in online global mode (action size = 3) against RL-based and classical PSO baselines.
These are compared against a broad range of traditional and adaptive PSO variants, including PSO-LDIW, FIPS, CLPSO, SAEPSO, and UAPSO, as well as reinforcement learning-based methods: RLAM-PSO (DDPG) \cite{Yin2023RLAMPSO}, DQN-PSO \cite{Aoun2024Deep}, SAC-SAPSO \cite{vonEschwege2024Soft}, MPSORL, and PPO-PSO.
To isolate the impact of each architectural component, we conduct an ablation study using three AFSACPSO variants: the full baseline, a variant without the Transformer encoder (No Attention), and a standard SAC without CrossQ optimizations (No CrossQ).
For each algorithm, we conduct $\symNumruns$ independent runs per scenario and report mean and standard deviation on the same endpoint used for optimization (\texttt{fitnessComponents.totalFitness}).
Inferential analysis uses two-sided paired t-tests as the primary test, with two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests as a robustness check.
Within each scenario, all pairwise p-values are adjusted using Holm--Bonferroni family-wise correction.
For each pair, we additionally report the paired mean difference with a 95\% confidence interval, Cohen's $d_z$, and rank-biserial correlation. Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams} summarises all hyperparameters used in the experiments. \begin{table}[htbp]
