The Full Baseline demonstrates steady improvement from episode 40 onward, reaching near-zero cumulative reward by episode 100.
This smooth convergence indicates that the retained controller receives a consistent learning signal once fitness improvements become regular. The ``No Attention'' variant's persistently lower rewards (often between $-50$ and $-90$) and higher variance throughout training confirm that temporal context from the state encoder is valuable for stable policy learning.
Without the attention mechanism's ability to aggregate historical swarm statistics, the agent struggles to identify effective parameter adaptation strategies. \subsection{Results Summary} The experimental results across 26 algorithm configurations and three benchmark scenarios reveal several key findings for adaptive PSO-based UAV path planning: RL Framework Selection. AFSACPSO achieves the lowest mean fitness in two of three scenarios among RL-based controllers, while MPSORL is best in Scenario 3.
Across runs, AFSACPSO has the lowest standard deviation in all three scenarios.
RLAMPSO remains competitive, but DQN-PSO degrades sharply with scenario complexity and shows the largest variance.
The maximum-entropy SAC objective appears to favor stability in the high-dimensional parameter space. Architectural Components. The refreshed ablation study indicates scenario-dependent behavior across Full, No Attention, and No CrossQ variants.
Under Holm-corrected paired tests, no Full-vs-variant contrast is significant in Scenarios 1--3, so component impacts should be interpreted as directional trends rather than confirmed causal gains.
This motivates larger-sample ablation sweeps before making stronger architectural claims. Comparison with Traditional Methods. SAEPSO achieves the lowest mean fitness in Scenarios 1 and 3, while SAEPSO* achieves the best mean in Scenario 2.
SAEPSO also yields the lowest variability, but AFSACPSO runs 7--8$\times$ faster due to inference.
Classical time-varying schedules (PSO-LDIW) remain strong baselines that should not be overlooked. Practitioner Recommendations. For deployment, AFSACPSO provides strong performance with fast inference, while SAEPSO is preferred when the best mean fitness is the priority and compute budget allows.
PSO-LDIW remains a strong alternative if RL training is not feasible. \section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion} The experimental results demonstrate that AFSACPSO effectively addresses the parameter adaptation challenge in PSO for complex UAV path planning.
This section interprets key findings, discusses limitations, and situates our contributions within the broader literature. \subsection{Interpretation of Results} The refreshed ablation results are mixed across scenarios and do not support a single universally dominant component.
The Full Baseline is strongest in Scenario 2, while the remaining ablation variants show scenario-dependent trade-offs in Scenarios 1 and 3.
Given that Holm-corrected pairwise tests are non-significant for all Full-vs-variant comparisons, these observations should be treated as directional evidence rather than definitive causal attribution. Compared to prior RL-PSO methods, our ablation results indicate improved training stability when CrossQ components are enabled, particularly when target networks are removed and critic batch normalization is retained.
Unlike RLAM-PSO \cite{Yin2023RLAMPSO} which relies on DDPG's deterministic policies, our entropy-regularized approach maintains exploration throughout training.
DQN-PSO's performance degradation in complex scenarios confirms that discretized parameter spaces fundamentally limit adaptation granularity. \subsection{Limitations} Several limitations warrant acknowledgment.
First, AFSACPSO requires offline training (3--14 hours depending on variant), which may not be feasible for rapidly changing mission profiles.
Second, our evaluation focused on static terrain maps; dynamic obstacles and time-varying threat zones remain untested.
Third, while the policy generalizes across the three benchmark scenarios, transfer to significantly different terrain types (e.g., urban canyons, open water) was not evaluated.
Fourth, the fitness function weights ($\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_5$) were hand-tuned; automatic weight adaptation could further improve performance.
Fifth, the retained binary reward design is intentionally simple; alternative reward-shaping strategies were not explored in the current repository.
Finally, the computational overhead of the Transformer encoder, while modest, may pose challenges for embedded UAV systems with limited onboard compute. \subsection{Practical Implications} For practitioners, AFSACPSO offers the best balance of path quality and computational efficiency when training time is available upfront.
For scenarios where RL training is infeasible, SAEPSO and PSO-LDIW remain strong alternatives.
The modular architecture allows practitioners to selectively enable components based on available compute: the Transformer encoder can be omitted for simpler terrains without significant performance loss. \section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion} This paper presented AFSACPSO, a deep reinforcement learning framework for adaptive PSO parameter control in UAV path planning.
The proposed method integrates three key innovations: (i) a CrossQ-enhanced Soft Actor-Critic agent \cite{Bhatt2024CrossQ} that eliminates target networks and improves sample efficiency through batch-normalized critics, (ii) a Transformer-based state encoder \cite{Vaswani2017Attention} that captures temporal swarm dynamics over a sliding window of $\symWindowsize$ iterations, and (iii) a direct fitness-improvement reward that keeps the learning signal aligned with the optimization endpoint. Extensive experiments on 3D terrain navigation benchmarks with no-fly zones demonstrated that AFSACPSO achieves the best mean fitness in two of three RL scenarios, while AFSACPSO provides the lowest run-to-run variance in our RL benchmark.
Against traditional baselines, AFSACPSO matches or approaches strong self-adaptive PSO variants while running at 7--8$\times$ lower inference cost than SAEPSO Full.
This efficiency advantage is practically significant: the policy requires no per-run learning, making AFSACPSO deployable in latency-sensitive or embedded UAV systems where online RL is infeasible.
Ablation studies with refreshed 30-run experiments show scenario-dependent trade-offs among components, and no Full-vs-variant pair reaches Holm-corrected significance in Scenarios 1--3. Future work should systematically investigate hyperparameter sensitivity for the retained controller, extend the state representation to natively handle dynamic obstacles, and explore meta-learning techniques to reduce the substantial offline training demands across diverse operational domains. \section*{Declaration of Competing Interests}
